WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.500
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about EFS,</v>

2
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.150
your Elastic File System.

3
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.820
So it's a managed network file system or NFS,

4
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.200
and the power of it

5
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.500
is that it can be mounted on many EC2 instances at a time.

6
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.750
So it works for EC2 instances

7
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.970
that are going to live within the same region

8
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.710
but in different availability zones,

9
00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.750
so that's why it's called a network file system.

10
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.020
And it's also going to work for your on-premises servers

11
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.190
if you mount them,

12
00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.680
and first, you need to connect your on-premise environment,

13
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.520
of course to AWS, using direct connect or VPN connection.

14
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.590
It's highly available, it's scalable.

15
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:32.430
It's quite expensive.

16
00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.100
It's about three times the cost of gp2,

17
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.230
and you're going to pay per gigabyte use,

18
00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.540
which I think is quite a cool model,

19
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.350
whereas for EBS, you have to pay per gigabytes provisions.

20
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.840
So your EFS file system will be living on AWS,

21
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.120
and you'll have a security group attached to it.

22
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.010
And your EC2 instances, for example,

23
00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.900
across three different AZs,

24
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.330
if you set up the security groups correctly,

25
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:58.320
can mount and access your EFS file system.

26
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.540
So if we look at it, the use cases for EFS

27
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.370
are going to be content management,

28
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.380
web data serving, data sharing.

29
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.570
WordPress is a really good candidate.

30
00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.780
And for it to work, you need to have a Linux-based AMI.

31
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.060
So Windows does not work with EFS.

32
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.500
It's very important to understand this

33
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.150
from an exam perspective.

34
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.330
And overall, the file system that you're going to use

35
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.130
must be POSIX-compliant,

36
00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:23.130
which is a good use case for Linux.

37
00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.640
It uses the NFSv4.1 protocol.

38
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.600
And to control access to EFS, you must use security groups.

39
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.150
You get encryption arrest using KMS,

40
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.940
and you can only attach it into one VPC,

41
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.350
and you create one mount target,

42
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.160
one elastic network interface, ENI, per AZ.

43
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.920
So as I said, it's POSIX file system, so that means Linux,

44
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.930
and it has a standard file API.

45
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.520
The file system will scale automatically,

46
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.020
which I think is really, really nice.

47
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.440
So you can scale from one gigabyte to terabytes, of course.

48
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.400
You pay per usage,

49
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.130
so you only pay for the number of gigabytes you have

50
00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.390
in your file system.

51
00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:58.980
So there is no capacity planning

52
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.870
happening behind the scenes.

53
00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.550
Now, the very important part about EFS

54
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.180
is to understand the performance and storage classes.

55
00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.330
Then we have different performance and storage classes.

56
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.550
So the first, the scale about EFS,

57
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.770
you get thousands of concurrent NFS clients

58
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.500
and 10 gigabytes plus of throughputs,

59
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.770
and you can grow to a Petabyte-scale network file system

60
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.050
automatically, which is really nice.

61
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.000
You can also set the performance mode

62
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.120
at the EFS network file system creation time,

63
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.200
and you have several options.

64
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.350
The first one is General Purpose, which is a default.

65
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.900
It's used for latency-sensitive use cases

66
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.720
such as a web server, a CMS, et cetera.

67
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.260
But if you wanted to maximize throughput, you have Max I/O,

68
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.080
which is a higher latency kind of network file system,

69
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.170
but higher throughput, and it's highly parallel.

70
00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.690
So it's great if you have big data applications

71
00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.030
or media processing needs.

72
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.280
Now, throughput mode, you have different options.

73
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.750
The first one is Bursting.

74
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.970
So you have one terabyte,

75
00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.830
means that it's 50 megabytes per second

76
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.100
plus burst up to 100 megabytes per second.

77
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.990
So that's the kind of bursting you get.

78
00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.400
You don't have to remember the numbers,

79
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.930
but just to give you an idea.

80
00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.690
Provisioned is when you want to set your throughput

81
00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.400
regardless of your storage size.

82
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.670
So the previous one was growing in throughput

83
00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.900
as we have more storage,

84
00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.110
but with Provisioned, you can have one gigabyte per second

85
00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.580
for one terabyte of storage,

86
00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.970
that is fine because you have de correlated

87
00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.340
your throughput from your storage.

88
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.680
And finally, to make things a little bit simpler,

89
00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:29.790
you have Elastic,

90
00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.460
to automatically scale the throughput up and down

91
00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.110
based on your workload.

92
00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.130
So, for example,

93
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.980
you can get up to 3 gigabytes per second for reads,

94
00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.320
and one gigabytes per second for writes

95
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.360
based on your workload.

96
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.280
And this is going to be a great fit

97
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.533
when you have unpredictable workloads.

98
00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.620
Now for storage classes, we have several options.

99
00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.670
We have Storage Tiers,

100
00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.250
which is a lifecycle management feature

101
00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.950
to allow you to move files to different source tiers

102
00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.800
after a certain number of days.

103
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.480
So you have the Standard tier

104
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.150
which is used for frequently accessed files,

105
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:07.800
and then you have the EFS-IA tier for infrequent access,

106
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.650
which gives you a cost to retrieve files,

107
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.010
but a lower price to store these files.

108
00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.770
And then you have the Archive storage tier.

109
00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:18.510
This is for rarely accessed data,

110
00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:21.450
so you only access, for example, data a few times a year,

111
00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.670
which is going to be a lot cheaper

112
00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.950
to store the data on that tier.

113
00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.120
To move your files automatically between your storage tiers,

114
00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.730
you can implement lifecycle policies

115
00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.380
which will allow to define after how many days

116
00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.510
a file should be moved to which tier.

117
00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.840
So here's an example in which we have files in EFS standard,

118
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.750
and one of these files hasn't been accessed for 60 days,

119
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.630
then by setting up the right lifecycle policy,

120
00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:50.460
we can move it to a new storage tier such as EFS-IA.

121
00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.580
Now, in terms of availability and durability,

122
00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.530
Standard is going to be great

123
00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.690
when you have a multi-AZ setup,

124
00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.840
so, your EFS across multiple availability zones,

125
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.990
which is great for your production workloads

126
00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.930
so that you're resistant to disasters.

127
00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.300
But if you just want to do development

128
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.490
and want to have cheaper options,

129
00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:12.900
then you choose One Zone,

130
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.140
which is going to give you only one availability zone,

131
00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.330
and you will still have backups on it.

132
00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:22.590
And it's also compatible with the IA type of storage tier,

133
00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:27.120
so you have the EFS One Zone-IA type of option.

134
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.930
Overall, by using the right EFS storage classes,

135
00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.540
you can do up to 90% in cost savings.

136
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.250
So this is very helpful.

137
00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.390
Now, EFS has a way to be mounted for on-premises servers

138
00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:40.650
and has VPC peering.

139
00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:42.810
So, your EFS has ENIs, okay,

140
00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.090
they're redundant across multiple AZ.

141
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.510
And then your VPC elsewhere can be peered

142
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.090
with your VPC of EFS for your EC2 instance

143
00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.580
to access your EFS drive,

144
00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.210
or also on-premises servers through direct connect

145
00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.730
or VPN, for site-to-site VPN,

146
00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:03.000
can also access your EBS volumes, okay?

147
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.220
And you can build redundancy, for example,

148
00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.140
by having direct connect to direct connect

149
00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.730
or direct connect to VPN.

150
00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.230
Now, when your on-premises server

151
00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.420
need to mount your EFS drives,

152
00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:16.620
they need to mount using the IPv4 of the ENIs, okay,

153
00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.000
they cannot use a DNS,

154
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.550
whereas your EC2 instance could use a DNS.

155
00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.680
So make sure that when you have on-premises servers,

156
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:24.930
retrieve the ENI IP,

157
00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:28.380
and then use that for mounting your NFS.

158
00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.510
Now, another feature you need to know about

159
00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.850
is around EFS access points.

160
00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.160
So this is to easily manage application access

161
00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:38.280
to NFS environments and enforce greater security.

162
00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.900
So for this, using these access points,

163
00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:44.040
we can define a user and a group on the POSIX file system

164
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.480
when accessing the file system.

165
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.379
So, this is going to allow us completely

166
00:06:48.379 --> 00:06:51.987
to restrict access to a directory within the file system

167
00:06:51.987 --> 00:06:55.230
and optionally specify a different route directory.

168
00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.360
And you can restrict access to these access points

169
00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:58.800
using IAM policies.

170
00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.100
So to make it very clear, here is my EFS file system,

171
00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.430
and it has the data file folder, the secret file folder,

172
00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:09.000
and the config folder directly within the route.

173
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.220
Now, if I set up an Access Point 1

174
00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.190
with UID 1001 and GID 1001,

175
00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.040
which is just a user ID and a group ID,

176
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.960
and the path is slash config,

177
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.210
then the users who have access

178
00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:23.100
to this access point using IAM,

179
00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.800
will only get access to the config directory

180
00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.870
within your EFS file system.

181
00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.700
But another group, for example,

182
00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.190
that has the analytics user groups,

183
00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:33.300
a different access points,

184
00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.300
so 1002 and 1002 for UID and group ID,

185
00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.950
then they only have access to the data file,

186
00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.136
and they can only access

187
00:07:42.136 --> 00:07:45.120
the data within this data folder, okay?

188
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.063
So again, all of this is managed through IAM permissions.

189
00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:51.930
Also, you can get file system policies

190
00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.660
and they are the same as S3 bucket policies,

191
00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.280
they're resource-based policies.

192
00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.920
And by default, you have full access to all clients.

193
00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.090
But here, for example,

194
00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.490
I can say that Stephane can only do

195
00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.440
mount and the write, okay,

196
00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.990
and must use secure transport,

197
00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:09.870
so must encrypt the connection into EFS.

198
00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.110
Next, we have cross-region replication for EFS,

199
00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:14.160
which allows you, of course,

200
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.770
to replicate objects from an EFS file system

201
00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.020
to another AWS region.

202
00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.810
And this works for existing file systems

203
00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.700
as well as a new file systems.

204
00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.260
So you don't need to recreate one

205
00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:27.240
if you wanted to enable this feature.

206
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.030
So you would have an EFS file system in us-east-1,

207
00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:31.410
and you would replicate it, for example,

208
00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.560
into eu-west-2 as a destination.

209
00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:37.920
It provides you RPO and RTO of minutes,

210
00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.900
which is great for disaster recovery,

211
00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.480
and it will not affect the provision throughput

212
00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.040
of the EFS file system.

213
00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.440
This is something that happens in the backend.

214
00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.140
So the use cases for cross-region replication

215
00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:51.930
is to meet your compliance and business continuity goals

216
00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.180
by having a second file system

217
00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.850
as an exact copy in another region.

218
00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.560
Okay. That's it for this lecture.

219
00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.560
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

